    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#acamapichtli"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acamapichtli.jpg" alt="Acamapichtli" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#acamapichtli">Acamapichtli</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acamapichtli,_-_1387,_1395_o_1403,_retrato_an%C3%B3nimo_(~1541).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Aztec Empire — more precisely the Triple Alliance, or Ēxcān Tlahtolōyān — had one of history's most unpromising beginnings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexica">Mexica</a> arrived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> as late-coming migrants and, by tradition, settled around 1325 CE on a small, swampy island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a> after seeing the prophesied omen of an eagle perched on a cactus devouring a serpent. They were among the weakest groups in the valley — latecomers who received the island because no established power wanted it — and served as tributaries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenaries</a> of the dominant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tepanecs">Tepanec</a> city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a>. Over a century of patient subordination they built their island into the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chinampa">chinampas</a> (raised-bed gardens in the lake) that transformed agricultural productivity, established their first royal dynasty under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acamapichtli">Acamapichtli</a> (r. c. 1375–1395), and earned a reputation for ferocity as mercenary warriors. The founding moment of the empire came from an external crisis: the death of the long-reigning Tepanec ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a> in 1426 triggered a succession struggle during which his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maxtla">Maxtla</a> reportedly had the Mexica ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chimalpopoca">Chimalpopoca</a> murdered. The new Mexica ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a> formed a coalition with the exiled Texcocan prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a> and the city-state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tlacopan">Tlacopan</a> against Maxtla's Azcapotzalco. Victory in 1428 was followed by the formal constitution of the Triple Alliance, which traditionally divided tribute two-fifths each to Tenochtitlan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#texcoco">Texcoco</a> and one-fifth to Tlacopan. At the same time the statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tlacaelel">Tlacaelel</a> reshaped Mexica history to supply the theological foundation for empire: the Mexica were recast as the chosen people of the sun and war god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a>, whose continued existence required the nourishment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, making perpetual war a cosmic necessity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan.jpg" alt="Tenochtitlan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_completa_de_Tenochtitl%C3%A1n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">thomas kole, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Aztec imperial economy was one of the most sophisticated tribute systems in pre-Columbian history, channelling the agricultural and craft surpluses of conquered regions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> through a highly organised system of collection, redistribution and long-distance commerce. The heartland in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> was transformed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chinampa">chinampas</a> — artificial plots built up from lake sediment and anchored by willow trees — which yielded several harvests a year and helped support a Tenochtitlan population estimated at 200,000 or more, making it one of the largest cities in the world. Beyond the valley, the 38 tribute provinces recorded in the Codex Mendoza supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cacao">cacao</a>, precious feathers, gold dust, jaguar skins and countless other goods. Much of this wealth passed through the great market of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a>, Tenochtitlan's twin city, whose scale and variety astonished the Spanish conquistador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bernal-diaz-del-castillo">Bernal Díaz del Castillo</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pochteca">pochteca</a>, a hereditary merchant class with its own patron deity, courts and social organisation, conducted long-distance trade beyond the imperial frontiers, gathering intelligence as well as goods. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flower-wars">Flower Wars</a> — ritualised combat against the independent city-states of Tlaxcala, Huexotzinco and Cholula — served the dual purpose of supplying captives for sacrifice and keeping the warrior class in readiness. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a> (r. 1502–1520) tightened central control over tribute and appointments and intervened in the succession at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#texcoco">Texcoco</a>, concentrating wealth and power in Tenochtitlan while deepening the resentments that would undermine imperial solidarity when the Spanish arrived.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ahuitzotl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl.jpg" alt="Ahuitzotl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahuitzotl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Aztec military was among the most formidable fighting forces of the pre-Columbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>, built on universal male military training, elite warrior orders and a style of warfare that prized capture over killing. Every <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexica">Mexica</a> boy received military training, and social advancement was explicitly tied to the number of captives taken in battle: a warrior who seized four enemies attained the prestigious rank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tequihua">tequihua</a>, opening the way to the elite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eagle-and-jaguar-warriors">Eagle and Jaguar warrior societies</a>. This capture-oriented warfare — driven by the religious demand for living sacrificial victims — set Aztec tactics apart from European battlefield practice and would prove a critical weakness against Spanish opponents who fought to kill. The army could mobilise very large forces, with the warriors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> and its allies supplemented by levies from tributary provinces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a> (r. 1486–1502), the eighth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huey-tlatoani">Huey Tlatoani</a>, was the greatest Aztec conqueror, extending the empire south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#xoconusco">Xoconusco</a> (Soconusco) on the Pacific coast of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chiapas">Chiapas</a>, near the present-day border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guatemala">Guatemala</a>, and marking the rededication of the enlarged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a> in 1487 with a mass sacrifice reported to have lasted four days. The army's clearest limitation was exposed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> (Tarascan) state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aztec-purepecha-war-of-1479">Aztec–Purépecha war of 1479</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#axayacatl">Axayacatl</a>'s force, reportedly 24,000 strong, was routed — fixing a western frontier the Aztecs never subsequently crossed. The fundamental strategic weakness of the system was its relationship with its tributaries: the coercive extraction that financed the army also created a vast pool of resentful subject peoples who needed only a credible alternative leadership to defect, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> provided in 1519.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#moctezuma-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii.jpg" alt="Moctezuma II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moctezuma_Mendoza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aztec government combined an elaborate ritual monarchy with a capable administration that managed tribute collection, trade, justice and religious ceremony across an empire of roughly 200,000 square kilometres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huey-tlatoani">Huey Tlatoani</a> — Great Speaker — of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> was not a hereditary monarch in the European sense but was chosen by a council of senior nobles from among eligible male members of the royal lineage, a system that in theory selected for ability but in practice concentrated power within a small elite and produced recurring succession disputes. Beneath him stood the cihuacoatl, a chief minister who managed internal affairs; tribute stewards (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#calpixque">calpixque</a>) who collected and recorded tribute in the provinces; and the senior commanders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tlacatecatl">tlacatecatl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tlacochcalcatl">tlacochcalcatl</a>. The judicial system maintained separate courts for nobles and commoners, with a high court in Tenochtitlan hearing the most serious cases. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a> carried out the most significant restructuring of Aztec government since the Triple Alliance's founding: he concentrated power in Tenochtitlan, replaced many officials appointed by his predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a> with members of the high nobility, and elaborated the ritual and ceremonial dimensions of royal authority to an unprecedented degree, presiding over a court of extraordinary complexity. The fundamental weakness was the empire's relationship with its subject peoples: unlike the Achaemenid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persians">Persians</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-empire">Romans</a>, the Aztecs made little systematic effort to integrate conquered populations into a shared imperial identity. Tributary peoples paid taxes, provided military service and honoured Aztec religious requirements but remained culturally and politically distinct, retaining their languages, local rulers and identities — and their resentments.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#florentine-codex"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florentine-codex.jpg" alt="Florentine Codex" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_florentino_51_9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernardino de Sahagún, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Aztec Empire's historical legacy was permanently shaped by its sudden and total defeat, which made it the defining case study of colonial conquest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a> and ensured that its civilisation — its cities, religious practices, artistic traditions and language — reached the wider world largely through the accounts of its conquerors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, one of the largest and most sophisticated cities on earth in 1519, was razed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico-city">Mexico City</a> built on its ruins, an act of symbolic obliteration matched by the systematic destruction of Aztec religious and historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a> by Spanish clergy. The surviving codices, and the testimony of indigenous informants compiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bernardino-de-sahagun">Fray Bernardino de Sahagún</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a>, provide the primary window into Aztec civilisation, and they record a world of great complexity: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> poetry, sophisticated astronomical and calendrical knowledge, elaborate philosophical and cosmological systems, and an urban planning tradition that astonished European observers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tributary-system">tributary system</a>, paradoxically, preserved more of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>'s political and cultural diversity than the Aztecs' reputation as conquerors might suggest: by governing through local rulers rather than replacing them, the empire left in place administrative structures and traditions that the Spanish colonial system could later co-opt. Nahuatl, already widespread and further spread by Aztec power, became the most important indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, used by missionaries as a principal vehicle for evangelisation across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mexico">Mexico</a>. The demographic catastrophe caused by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#smallpox">smallpox</a> and other European diseases — which killed an estimated 80 to 90 per cent of central Mexico's indigenous population within a century of contact — was not a consequence of Aztec rule, but it was among the most consequential events in the history of the Americas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hernan-cortes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes.jpg" alt="Hernán Cortés" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Hern%C3%A1n_Cort%C3%A9s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artistUnknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fall of the Aztec Empire was one of the most rapid in imperial history: from November 1519, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hernan-cortes">Cortés</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>'s guest, to the fall of the city in August 1521 was less than two years. The speed of collapse was produced by four mutually reinforcing forces that no pre-Columbian political system had been built to withstand. The first and most devastating was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#smallpox">smallpox</a>: the epidemic that swept through Tenochtitlan after the Spanish retreat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#la-noche-triste">La Noche Triste</a> (30 June–1 July 1520) killed a large share of the city's population within months, including the new ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuitlahuac">Cuitlahuac</a>, who had driven the Spanish from the city — stripping away the leadership and manpower needed for sustained resistance. The second was the mass defection of subject and enemy peoples: by some estimates Cortés's army at the final siege numbered as many as 100,000 warriors, of whom fewer than 1,000 were Spaniards; the great majority were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huexotzinca">Huexotzincas</a>, Chalcans, Texcocan factions and others who seized the opportunity to destroy the power that had dominated or threatened them. The third was the seizure of Moctezuma II, whose hesitancy in the face of the Spanish presence — whether from religious uncertainty about their identity or a calculated political gamble — removed effective central leadership at the most critical moment. The fourth was the structural contradiction of the tribute system itself, which had built a vast empire without creating the institutional loyalty, cultural integration or shared identity needed to sustain it once central authority was challenged. Tenochtitlan fell on 13 August 1521 after a siege of nearly three months; the last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuauhtemoc">Cuauhtémoc</a>, was captured fleeing by canoe, and the city was levelled.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Aztec Empire's collapse in 1521 — one of the swiftest falls from peak to extinction in the historical record — stands as the defining case study of hegemonic empires built on coercion rather than integration: the vast army that destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> was composed overwhelmingly of indigenous peoples whom the Aztecs had subjugated or fought, demonstrating that the empire's greatest structural vulnerability was not Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#steel">steel</a> but the accumulated resentments of its own tributaries and neighbours.</em></p>

